|> Exercise
    handle=_vertical_velocity_plot_

    Plot the vertical velocity of 
    |> del
        an object
    \ a mosquito whose height over time is
    given by this graph (use the same time interval
    as the graph):

    |> Image
        src=images/1.svg

    |> Solution

        Here is the “official” graph of the (vertical)
        velocity:

        |> Image
            src=images/64.svg

        On each interval, the velocity is the rate of
        change of the height, i.e., the _slope_ of
        the height graph. For example, it is

        $$
        {1\te{m} \over 1\te{s}} = 1\te{m}/\te{s}
        $$

        between $-4$s and $-3$s, where the mosquito
        goes up by one meter in one second.

        |> Pause

        _Note 1._ An empty circle
        !! As explained in Chapter >>functions, an empty circle of
        !! this type

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_empty_circle_alone.svg

        indicates a “missing” value. Here, the vertical velocity is _undefined_
        wherever the graph of the height has a sharp
        corner. (Because the slope of the graph is not
        well-defined at such corners.)

        |> Pause

        _Note 2._
        From $2$s to $2.5$s, the
        slope is

        $$ {-2\rt{0.05}\te{m} \over 0.5\rt{0.05}\te{s}} = -\rt{0.07}4\rt{0.1}\te{m}/\te{s} $$

        and similarly from
        $2.5$s to $3$s the slope is

        $$ {2\rt{0.05}\te{m} \over 0.5\rt{0.05}\te{s}} = 4\rt{0.1}\te{m}/\te{s} $$

        because $2/0.5 = 4$. (Think: _how many times_
        does $0.5$ go into $2$?)
